S20 P0 — Propose info layer (ticket #99) · bidirectionality verification log
============================================================================

Discipline: per NEW production behavior, break the production code, show a
GENUINE vitest FAIL (the test caught it), restore, show green. A test that
cannot fail proves nothing. Four new behaviors → four break/restore cycles.
All runs in the worktree .worktrees/s20-p0 on branch feature/s20-p0.

Baseline: `npx vitest run src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts` → 32 passed.
Post-restore identity: `diff` of chain-builder-adapter.ts against the
pre-break backup → IDENTICAL (byte-for-byte).

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Cycle 1 — Depth is LONGEST-path, not shortest-path (Axis 2)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Behavior: depthsFromTarget assigns each item the LONGEST path from the target,
so no producer renders shallower than any consumer. On the diamond-with-shortcut
fixture, Base is reachable at depth 1 (shortcut base→top) and depth 2
(base→left→top). Longest-path must pick 2.

Break: in depthsFromTarget's relaxation, replace the max-relaxation
  `if (candidate > (depth.get(producer) ?? -1))`
with a set-once (shortest/BFS) guard
  `if (!depth.has(producer))`.

Test: "assigns longest-path depth (target T0; base T2 despite the shortcut)"
Result: FAIL (genuine) —
  AssertionError: expected 1 to be 2 // Object.is equality
  - Expected: 2
  + Received: 1
The shortcut edge won under shortest-path; Base collapsed to depth 1.

Restore: revert to the max-relaxation guard.
Result: PASS.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Cycle 2 — proposalMetrics sums machineCount across the subtree (Axis 1/4)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Behavior: proposalMetrics.machineCount is the Σ of every stage's machineCount
(bigint, exact) — the cost sheet's Σ MACHINES line.

Break: delete the accumulation line `machineCount += stage.machineCount;` from
the proposalMetrics loop (leaving it at its 0n init).

Test: "sums power + machines + raw exactly over the whole proposal"
Result: FAIL (genuine) —
  expect(m.machineCount).toBe(5n)
  - Expected: 5n
  + Received: 0n

Restore: reinstate the accumulation line.
Result: PASS.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Cycle 3 — feeds names EVERY direct consumer (fan-out, Axis 2)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Behavior: a producer's `feeds` lists ALL its direct consumers — on a DAG a
fan-out producer (Base → Top, Left, Right) is legible only through this suffix.
The consumersOf map appends each consumer.

Break: replace the append
  `const list = ...; if (list === undefined) set([to]); else list.push(to);`
with an overwrite `consumersOf.set(link.fromItemId, [link.toItemId]);` — keeping
only the LAST consumer per producer.

Test: "names every direct consumer in feeds (fan-out shows both consumers)"
Result: FAIL (genuine) —
  expect(feedsByName.get("Base")).toEqual(["Top", "Left", "Right"])
  Base's fan-out collapsed to the single last consumer.

Restore: reinstate the append.
Result: PASS.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Cycle 4 — candidateCount is the real alternates count (Axis 3)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Behavior: each row's candidateCount = candidateRecipesFor(catalog, itemId).length
(0 or ≥2 by construction) — the "N recipes" chip / P1 picker hook.

Break: hardcode the row field to `candidateCount: 0`.

Test: "counts candidates per item (0 or >=2 by construction) from the catalog"
Result: FAIL (genuine) —
  expect(countByName.get("Ingot")).toBe(2)
  Ingot (default + alternate) reported 0 under the hardcode.

Restore: reinstate `candidateRecipesFor(catalog, s.itemId).length`.
Result: PASS.

